index 0000000..7843e46
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,186 @@
+# Deployment
+
+Fizzy can run on a single Linux server with [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org/).
+The web server, background jobs, SQLite databases, and uploaded files all live
+in one container, and SSL is handled by `kamal-proxy`.
+
+This is the setup most people want for a personal or small-team Fizzy: one box,
+one container, no external services beyond your mail server and a container
+registry.
+
+## Requirements
+
+* A Linux server with Docker installed (Kamal can install Docker for you) and
+  SSH access, ideally as `root`.
+* A hostname with an `A` (and optionally `AAAA`) DNS record pointing at the
+  server. Let's Encrypt needs the hostname to resolve before the first deploy.
+* Ports 80 and 443 open on the server, so `kamal-proxy` can obtain an SSL
+  certificate and serve HTTPS.
+* Docker and `git` on the machine you deploy from.
+* An SMTP server for outgoing mail. Fizzy sends magic-link sign-in codes and
+  notifications by email, so this is required.
+
+Kamal's default configuration in this repository uses a **local registry**: the
+image is built on your machine and pushed to a temporary registry that Kamal
+starts locally and tunnels to the server. That means you don't need a Docker Hub
+or GHCR account. If you prefer an external registry, see
+[Using an external registry](#using-an-external-registry) below.
+
+## Setup
+
+1. **Fork and clone the repository.** Then install the local dependencies:
+
+   ```sh
+   bin/setup
+   ```
+
+   (If you only want to deploy, `bundle install` is enough.)
+
+2. **Fill in `config/deploy.yml`.** The values you must change are:
+
+   * `servers.web` — your server's IP address.
+   * `proxy.host` and `APP_HOST` — the hostname users will visit, e.g.
+     `fizzy.example.com`. Both values must match.
+   * `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` — the `From` address for Fizzy email.
+   * `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, and `SMTP_USERNAME` — your mail server.
+
+   Fizzy uses STARTTLS and password authentication by default. If your mail
+   server uses implicit TLS (usually port 465), also add `SMTP_SSL: true` and
+   `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS: false` under `env.clear`.
+
+   If your server is ARM (for example AWS Graviton or a Hetzner CAX instance),
+   change `builder.arch` from `amd64` to `arm64`.
+
+3. **Generate your Rails credentials.** This creates `config/master.key` and
+   `config/credentials.yml.enc` with a generated `secret_key_base`:
+
+   ```sh
+   bin/rails credentials:edit
+   ```
+
+   Save and close the editor. You don't need to add anything else unless you
+   prefer to keep other secrets there. Commit `config/credentials.yml.enc` to
+   your fork, but never commit `config/master.key`.
+
+4. **Fill in `.kamal/secrets`.** By default it reads:
+
+   * `RAILS_MASTER_KEY` from the `config/master.key` created above.
+   * `SMTP_PASSWORD` from the `SMTP_PASSWORD` environment variable.
+
+   Set the SMTP password in your shell before deploying, or edit
+   `.kamal/secrets` to read it from wherever you keep it. Don't commit real
+   secrets.
+
+   ```sh
+   export SMTP_PASSWORD=your-smtp-password
+   ```
+
+5. **Deploy.**
+
+   ```sh
+   bin/kamal setup
+   ```
+
+   Kamal bootstraps Docker on the server if needed, starts `kamal-proxy`,
+   builds and pushes the image, obtains the SSL certificate, and starts Fizzy.
+   The first deploy can take a few minutes.
+
+6. **Visit `https://your-hostname`.** Create an account and sign in using the
+   magic link that Fizzy emails you.
+
+Subsequent deploys use:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+## How data is stored
+
+Everything that needs to persist lives in `/rails/storage` inside the
+container:
+
+* `production.sqlite3` — the primary database.
+* `production_cache.sqlite3`, `production_queue.sqlite3`, and
+  `production_cable.sqlite3` — Solid Cache, Solid Queue, and Solid Cable.
+* `production/files/` — uploaded files and attachments.
+
+That directory is mounted from the `fizzy_storage` Docker volume, which is kept
+across deploys and container replacements, so your data survives
+`bin/kamal deploy` and `bin/kamal setup`. Removing the volume
+(`docker volume rm fizzy_storage`) deletes all Fizzy data.
+
+To back up the data, copy the volume contents somewhere else. For example:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app exec --reuse "tar -czf - /rails/storage" > fizzy-backup-$(date +%F).tar.gz
+```
+
+The database is safe to copy while the app is running with SQLite's backup
+semantics, but for a completely consistent backup, stop the container first.
+
+## Background jobs
+
+Fizzy uses [Solid Queue](https://github.com/rails/solid_queue), which stores
+jobs in the `production_queue.sqlite3` database. Because this is a one-box
+deployment, `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: true` is set in `config/deploy.yml`, so the
+job supervisor runs inside the Puma web process — no extra container or worker
+role is needed. Jobs are also persisted in SQLite, so they survive restarts.
+
+## SSL
+
+`kamal-proxy` terminates SSL and automatically requests a Let's Encrypt
+certificate for `proxy.host`. Rails is configured with `assume_ssl` and
+`force_ssl` in production, so it redirects to HTTPS and marks cookies as secure.
+
+Make sure the DNS record for your hostname points at the server *before*
+running `bin/kamal setup`; if certificate issuance fails, check that port 80 is
+reachable from the internet and run `bin/kamal proxy reboot`.
+
+## Using an external registry
+
+The default local registry is convenient, but it requires your machine to stay
+connected while the image is pushed and pulled. To use Docker Hub, GHCR, or
+another registry instead:
+
+1. Edit `config/deploy.yml`:
+
+   ```yaml
+   image: your-user/fizzy
+
+   registry:
+     server: ghcr.io # or registry.digitalocean.com/... etc.
+     username: your-user
+     password:
+       - KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD
+   ```
+
+2. Uncomment and fill in the `KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD` line in
+   `.kamal/secrets`, or export the variable in your shell.
+
+## Optional: web push notifications
+
+Fizzy can send browser push notifications if you provide VAPID keys. Generate a
+pair:
+
+```ruby
+vapid_key = WebPush.generate_key
+puts "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{vapid_key.private_key}"
+puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{vapid_key.public_key}"
+```
+
+Then add `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` and `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` to `env.secret` (and
+`.kamal/secrets`) and `env.clear` in `config/deploy.yml`, respectively, and
+redeploy. Push notifications are optional; the rest of Fizzy works without
+them.
+
+## Troubleshooting
+
+* **The deploy hangs while waiting for the app to become healthy.** Check the
+  logs with `bin/kamal app logs`. The most common cause is a missing or wrong
+  `RAILS_MASTER_KEY` in `.kamal/secrets` — Rails can't boot without a
+  `secret_key_base`.
+* **Emails aren't delivered.** Verify the `SMTP_*` values in
+  `config/deploy.yml` and the `SMTP_PASSWORD` secret, then check
+  `bin/kamal app logs` for SMTP errors.
+* **The certificate wasn't issued.** Confirm DNS resolves to the server, port
+  80 is open, and run `bin/kamal proxy reboot`.
